From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VIF1 UNPACK: fast paths for unmasked writes without row addition

What it is for:
  In Ben 10: Protector of Earth's first level, with enemies on screen, the
  VIF1/VU1 worker thread is the limit (it needs more than the 33 ms of a
  30-per-second frame). A Windows profile of that thread in a fight
  showed PS2Memory::processVIF1Data at 6.5% of its own samples: UNPACK
  wrote every vector through the per-field mask and mode loop, even when
  there is no write mask and no row addition.

The changes:
  - V4-32 with CL == WL, no write mask and MODE 0 or 3: the vectors are
    copied as they are to consecutive VU1 addresses, wrapping at the end
    of VU1 data memory (1024 qwords), in at most two copies.
  - Any other handled format with no write mask and no row addition
    (MODE 0 or 3, or V4-5, which never adds): each decompressed vector is
    stored whole, without the per-field loop.
  Same bytes in VU1 memory as before.

How it was checked:
  A throwaway program fed 200,000 random VIF1 packets (STCYCL with random
  CL and WL, STMOD, STMASK, STROW, STCOL, then one to four UNPACKs of every
  format, masked or not, with random NUM, addresses and the TOPS and
  unsigned bits) to PS2Memory built with and without this patch: the same
  VU1 data memory at every 100th packet. ps2xTest 454/454 (its VIF UNPACK,
  STCYCL, STMOD and mask cases included).

--- a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_vif1_interpreter.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_vif1_interpreter.cpp
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 // Based on Blackline Interactive implementation
+#include <algorithm>
 #include "runtime/ps2_memory.h"
 #include <cstring>
 
@@ -572,7 +573,26 @@
 
             const bool zeroExtend = (imm & 0x4000u) != 0u;
 
-            if (m_vu1Data && totalBytes > 0 && pos + totalBytes <= sizeBytes)
+            const uint32_t unpackMode = vif1_regs.mode & 3u;
+            if (m_vu1Data && totalBytes > 0 && pos + totalBytes <= sizeBytes && vl == 0u && vn == 3u && cl == wl &&
+                !maskEnable && (unpackMode == 0u || unpackMode == 3u))
+            {
+                // V4-32 with no write mask, no row addition and every
+                // cycle a write (CL == WL): each vector is copied as is to
+                // consecutive VU addresses, wrapping at the end of VU1 data
+                // memory (1024 qwords). The same bytes as the loop below
+                // writes one vector at a time, in two copies at most.
+                const uint8_t *srcBase = data + pos;
+                uint32_t done = 0u;
+                while (done < writeVectorCount)
+                {
+                    const uint32_t destVec = (vuAddr + done) & 0x3FFu;
+                    const uint32_t run = std::min<uint32_t>(writeVectorCount - done, 0x400u - destVec);
+                    std::memmove(m_vu1Data + destVec * 16u, srcBase + done * 16u, run * 16u);
+                    done += run;
+                }
+            }
+            else if (m_vu1Data && totalBytes > 0 && pos + totalBytes <= sizeBytes)
             {
                 const uint8_t *srcBase = data + pos;
                 uint32_t srcIndex = 0u;
@@ -720,6 +740,14 @@
 
                     const bool canAdd = (vl != 3u || vn != 3u);
                     const uint32_t mode = vif1_regs.mode & 3u;
+                    if (decoded && handledFormat && !maskEnable && (!canAdd || mode == 0u || mode == 3u))
+                    {
+                        // No write mask and no row addition: every field
+                        // takes the decompressed value (the loop below gives
+                        // the same in this case).
+                        std::memcpy(m_vu1Data + destOff, decompressed, sizeof(decompressed));
+                        continue;
+                    }
                     const uint32_t colIdx = (cyclePos > 3u) ? 3u : cyclePos;
                     const uint32_t maskCycle = (cyclePos > 3u) ? 3u : cyclePos;
 
